The idea that:
```html
<script type="css-module" specifier="/foo.css">
  #content { 
    color: red; 
  }
</script>
<my-element> 
   <template shadowrootmode="open" adoptedstylesheets="/foo.css"> 
     <!-- ... -->
   </template>
</my-element>
```
Is an almost 100% declarative translation of:
```js
import style from '/foo.css' with { type: 'css' };

class MyElement extends HTMLElement {
  constructor() {
    super();
    this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });
    this.shadowRoot.adoptedStyleSheets = [ style ];
    this.shadowRoot.innerHTML = '...';
  }
}

customElements.define('my-element', MyElement);
```
Is incredibly exciting to the Web Components Community Group and the greater community made up by its members' various networks. It's seemingly only a few steps away from fully declarative custom elements, which seem just as interesting to implementors as they do to the developing communities served by the hard work those implementors are putting into browsers. The way it implies a future syntax for other module scriptable content types is also quite nice, and quite needed in the content of each (JSON, CSS, HTML, WASM, et al).

If there is anything the WCCG can do to support moving the conversation around this API forward (testing [we've been working on WPT coverage for a number of features as of late and are happy to expand out efforts for such an important API], feedback [point us to the right place], demos [we actually had a series of presentation in the Spring around [Declarative Custom Elements](https://github.com/w3c/webcomponents-cg/discussions?discussions_q=is%3Aopen+DCE+Demo) concepts, many of which featured the need for an API like this], etc.), please let us know!

I'm not sure a global map would be beneficial to a page level developer achieve their goals in that not populating the module graph with `/foo.css` (or similar) would prevent `<my-other-element>` from being able to benefit from the shared code in the CSS module script later in the lifecycle of the page, seemingly?

```js
import style from '/foo.css' with { type: 'css' };

class MyOtherElement extends HTMLElement {
  constructor() {
    super();
    this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' });
    this.shadowRoot.adoptedStyleSheets = [ style ];
    this.shadowRoot.innerHTML = '...';
  }
}

customElements.define('my-other-element', MyElement);
```

While I could envision paths by which a global ID map were bent to the will of the developer in this case, it's hard to see a path that does not require a developer to more heavily rely on tooling in that the ability to convert the standard code of:

```js
import style from '/foo.css' with { type: 'css' };

// elide complexity

this.shadowRoot.adoptedStyleSheets = [ style ];
```

to something like:

```js
this.shadowRoot.adoptedStyleSheets = [ someMagicMapBoundToWindow.get('foo.css') ];
```

This wide separation of what is written and what is shipped seems like a lot when the module graph already exists exactly for this sort of responsibility.

On the topic of other module types, some feel that even including them in the explainer is preparing it for derision (due to an even broader expanse of unanswered questions). However, there is specific reference to [HTML module scripts](https://github.com/MicrosoftEdge/MSEdgeExplainers/blob/main/ShadowDOM/explainer.md#:~:text=inline%20version%20of-,HTML%20module%20scripts,-that%20would%20be) in the explainer. I greatly agree that clarifying that the `specifier` attribute can apply across module types (include JS itself!) and how would be a great way to solidify the benefits of this approach!

@domenic does bring up a question I hadn't seen before about the mutability of the `specifier` attribute itself.
- Should it be some sort of magic attribute wherein it can only be set with side effects once? If possible, feels like a cheat code!
- Is there a path to removing something from the module graph? This would allow it to just be live, but the cascade effects of things no longer having resolved values seems like it opens even more questions.
- Should it be "write only" to the module graph? Meaning it populates every value that it is ever set to with its contents, were the value not otherwise available in the graph. This does align with some of the questions about possible spamming of the module graph at TPAC.

Has it ever been suggest to reverse the setup; why not `inject` instead of `adopt`? 

```html
<link rel="inject stylesheet" href="custom-element.framework.css" type="text/css; components=custom-element, another-element">
<link rel="inject stylesheet" href="custom-element.overwrite.css" type="text/css; components=custom-element">
<custom-element></custom-element>

<style rel="inject stylesheet" type="text/css; components=custom-element">
:host {
  color: green;
}
</style>
<custom-element></custom-element>
```

The key difference is that the `<link>` or `<style>` decides its destination rather than `<template>` that adopts a module through an `shadowrootadoptedstylesheets` attribute or a  `<link>` tag. You could use the `type` attribute to define which component to target. Like `charset=UTF-8`, you define a specific characteristic for the mime type. 

The `<link>` and `<style>` tags with `rel="inject stylesheet"` could be anywhere on the page. If they are above the first custom-element: no FOUC, if after than probably a FOUC.

You could say the above is a declarative solutions for the following.

```js
const injectableSheets = document.querySelectorAll('style[rel="inject stylesheet"]');
for (const injectableSheet of injectableSheets) {
  const sheet = new CSSStyleSheet();
  sheet.replaceSync(injectableSheet.textContent);

  const parsedMime = parseMime(injectableSheet.type);
  const components = document.querySelectorAll(parsedMime.components);
  for (const component of components) {
    component.shadowRoot.adoptedStyleSheets.push(sheet);
  }
}

const injectableLinks = document.querySelectorAll('link[rel="inject stylesheet"]');
for (const injectableLink of injectableLinks) {
  const sheet = new CSSStyleSheet();
  const resp = await fetch(injectableLink.href);
  sheet.replaceSync(await resp.text());

  const parsedMime = parseMime(injectableSheet.type);
  const components = document.querySelectorAll(parsedMime.components);
  for (const component of components) {
    component.shadowRoot.adoptedStyleSheets.push(sheet);
  }
}
```

In my opinion this simplifies the setup:
- It's like defining reset css for custom-elements. It's crystal clear to developers.
- The solution targets the custom element, more precisely its shadow root, not the shadow root contents
- A template tag != a shadow root, which is what the current solution is basically opening up to. How many `shadowroot*` attributes do you want to invent?
- It circumvents the whole `specifier` discussion: the custom-element tag name is the specifier. No modules or importmaps required.
- With the current adopt suggestion, there might be a solution to adopt stylesheets with DSD, when (also) creating new custom elements dynamically through `document.createElement('custom-element')`, the creator of the element must call `.shadowRoot.adoptedStyleSheets.push()` or the element itself must have a solution built-in for when it is created within a dynamic context. The inject solution solves both DSD and dynamic contexts.
- It's a declarative solution for very simplistic javascript.
- No additional tag/attribute required, it relies on known semantics. The `rel` attribute defines that it should not be injected into the full document  but rather to a specific subset of the document.
- It could be extended by adding more possible parameters to the (mime) `type` attribute, e.g. `text/css; fragment=template-id`.

@annevk - that is true that declarative custom elements can reduce style duplication within a single component definition. However, there is still a need to share common styles *between* components, which this proposal enables.

Consider a general branding look-and-feel with a set of predefined fonts, backgrounds, padding, etc. in a stylesheet. Without this proposal, every custom element definition would need to redefine these identical styles. So I do see a use case for this proposal alongside declarative custom elements.

For the "big tree of nodes" situation, this approach could be extended to HTML elements. I cover this in the [Other Declarative Modules](https://github.com/MicrosoftEdge/MSEdgeExplainers/blob/main/ShadowDOM/explainer.md#other-declarative-modules) section of the explainer.

> Nicolò confirmed that the `data:` URL approach does not work. They are at least observable through the identity of the returned `CSSStyleSheet` object. I don't think we can require this particular implementation as it's rather inefficient.

@annevk  - could you elaborate? Is your concern only with efficiency? 

Which `CSSStyleSheet`'s identity are you referring to? Note in this case:

```
<script type="importmap">
{
  "imports": {
    "foo": "data:text/css,div {color:blue}"
  }
}
</script>
<script type="module">
  import styles1 from "foo" with { type: "css"};
  import styles2 from "foo" with { type: "css"};
  alert(styles1 === styles2);
  document.adoptedStyleSheets = [styles1];
</script>
```

...the above alerts "true" in both Chromium and Firefox Nightly with `layout.css.module-scripts.enabled = true` set in `about:config`, which means there's only one `CSSStyleSheet` instance when importing multiple times. I made a CodePen of this here: https://codepen.io/Kurt-Catti-Schmidt/pen/yyeorZp (note this logs to `console.log`, so you need to open CodePen's console to see `true`).

If you are referring to the identity of a `CSSStyleSheet` attached to `<style>` tag (via `LinkStyle`), that doesn't get created in this case, so it'll always be empty for declarative modules. It only gets created in the existing "The update a style block algorithm", which requires `type` is empty or "text/css".

@annevk - you mentioned using blobs in the PR instead of dataURI's. I hadn't considered that approach before, but it seems really nice!

Were you thinking of something like this? https://codepen.io/Kurt-Catti-Schmidt/pen/YPwreNX?editors=1111

This would sidestep any potential concerns about performance with dataURI's, and also avoid needing to URL-encode. I'll update my explainer and prototypes with this approach.

> This is pretty common. See, for example, the sharing done in the popular component library web-awesome.

In our (private) web component library we have a similar solution, only the property is called `adopt`. @jyasskin did mention that the `host-for` (or `adopt`) attribute on the host [is not going to be an improvement](https://github.com/w3ctag/design-reviews/issues/1195#issuecomment-4608033622), I am still wondering why. No reason has been given. Why not use a static property on the class together with an attribute on the host having the same name as the class property to define the styles of the element? 

With the current proposal it will be (way) easier to overwrite stylesheets for a declarative Shadow DOM compared to an imperative Shadow DOM, because when creating declaratively you can decide per instance which styles you are going to add to the `shadowrootadoptedstylesheets` attribute. When creating an element dynamically, you would have to modify adopted stylesheets after it was populated. Maybe you could modify the static class properties, but that might not be possible.

#### Full control declaratively.
```html
<wa-button>
  <!-- I can do whatever I want, I have full control here -->
   <template shadowrootmode="open" shadowrootadoptedstylesheets="wa-button wa-variant my-size-overwrite">
   </template>
</wa-button>
```

#### Limited control dynamically.
```js
import myStyles from 'my-size-overwrite';

const element  = document.createElement('wa-button');
// element.shadowRoot.adoptedStyleSheets are already loaded/imported here, I need to filter wa-size here, but how should I do that, CSSStyleSheet does not have a name/URI
element.shadowRoot.adoptedStyleSheets = [...element.shadowRoot.adoptedStyleSheets.filter(() => true || false), myStyles];
```
a wrapped reset of the static property could provide a solution

```js
import WaButton from 'web-awesome/wa-button';
import buttonStyles from 'web-awesome/styles/wa-button' with { type: "css" };
import variantStyles from 'web-awesome/styles/wa-styles' with { type: "css" };
import mySizeOverwrite from 'my-size-overwrite' with { type: "css" };

const css = WaButton.css;
try {
  WaButton.css = [];
  const element  = document.createElement('wa-button');
  // but now I also need to import the custom element styles if I 
  element.shadowRoot.adoptedStyleSheets = [buttonStyles, myStyles, buttonStyles, mySizeOverwrite];
} finally {
  WaButton.css = css;
}
```

But what if the property is a `static get css() {}`?

### Solution

An attribute on the host together with a static property on the class solves this issue.

```ts
import styles from 'web-awesome/styles/wa-button';
import buttonStyles from 'web-awesome/styles/wa-button' with { type: "css" };
import variantStyles from 'web-awesome/styles/wa-styles' with { type: "css" };
import sizeStyles from 'web-awesome/styles/wa-sizes' with { type: "css" };

@customElement('wa-button')
export default class WaButton extends WebAwesomeFormAssociatedElement {
  static adopt = [styles, variantStyles, buttonStyles, sizeStyles];
}
```

```js
import WaButton from 'web-awesome/wa-button';
import buttonStyles from 'web-awesome/styles/wa-button' with { type: "css" };
import variantStyles from 'web-awesome/styles/wa-styles' with { type: "css" };
import mySizeOverwrite from 'my-size-overwrite' with { type: "css" };

document.createElement('wa-button'); // takes adopt static property
document.createElement('wa-button', { adopt:  [buttonStyles, myStyles, buttonStyles, mySizeOverwrite] }); //overwrites the adopt static property for this instance
document.createElement('wa-button', { adopt: [...WaButton.adopt, mySizeOverwrite] }); //just add my overwrites, overwrites the adopt static property for this instance
```

and declaratively

```html
<!-- I can still do whatever I want, I have full control here -->
<wa-button adopt="wa-button wa-variant my-size-overwrite">
   <template shadowrootmode="open">
   </template>
</wa-button>
```

> @frederikbosch - how would this proposal work when custom elements are not used?

With my proposal, it comes down to *when* the `adopt` attribute/option will be materialized. This materialization should take place when the Shadow DOM is attached to the element, whether that is a custom or built-in element.

```js
const div = document.createElement('div', { adopt: [buttonStyles] }); // creates a div that carries the adopt
const shadow = div.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' }); // materializes the adopt
console.log(shadow.adoptedStylesheets); // [buttonStyles]
```

```html
<div adopt="my-styles">
  <!-- materializes the adopt -->
  <template shadowrootmode="open"></template>
</div>

<div adopt="my-styles">
  <!-- this element without a ShadowDOM carries the adopt, but materialization only takes place once a Shadow DOM is attached (dynamically) -->
</div>
```

Since a built-in element does not have a static adopt property, there is nothing to adopt by default. For a custom element, what should be carried should be decided when the element is parsed or when `document.createElement` is called.

```js
class MyElement extends HTMLElement {
  static adopt = ['my-styles'];

  constructor() {
    super();
    // at this point, after calling super(), it is known which stylesheets are going to be adopted when attachShadow() is called
    // so the super() call constructs what is to be carried
    // if the adopt option is passed to document.createElement it uses that, otherwise it fetches the value of the adopt static class property

    this.attachShadow({ mode: 'open' }); // materializes the adopt
  }
}
```

Addition: which use case do you have for this? Well we have a feed exactly as in the Reddit example. The initial state of the feed is directly embedded in the HTTP response. The feed in the response is a DSD that includes messages which are also a DSD. From that initial state, new messages are fetched (window.fetch) and converted into new custom elements (`document.createElement`) and attached to the feed.

